WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:02.870
There's an idea in science called
the null hypothesis.

00:00:02.870 --> 00:00:05.810
And it works like this:
when you're setting out to prove a theory,

00:00:05.810 --> 00:00:08.530
your default answer should be,
"It's not going to work,"

00:00:08.530 --> 00:00:12.530
and you've got to convince the world otherwise
through clear results.

00:00:12.530 --> 00:00:17.690
So in my last video, while I was trying to
talk to a camera up on that zero-g flight,

00:00:17.690 --> 00:00:21.170
the student researchers
all through the rest of the plane,

00:00:21.170 --> 00:00:24.380
who were physicists, psychologists, chemists,
electrical engineers,

00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:28.190
all of them were trying to disprove their
null hypothesis.

00:00:28.190 --> 00:00:34.460
We want to see if brain-computer interfaces can
be used for very extreme situations, such as space.

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:39.620
and that zero-g or weightlessness will not
really interfere with the signalling,

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:41.630
and if it does, how can we filter it out?

00:00:41.630 --> 00:00:46.010
In the flights, the participants are going
to play a game called "shoot-the-alien"

00:00:46.010 --> 00:00:48.280
and they'll do so with their brain activity.

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:52.340
Simply put, they'll just think of movements
and not actually move

00:00:52.340 --> 00:00:56.160
and by thinking of that movement, they'll
control a little cannon on a screen.

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:58.540
If I really move my right hand,
I get a certain brain signal

00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:03.360
but if I imagine moving my right hand,
I get the exact same brain signal.

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:07.310
That means that I can learn a computer how
to respond to those brain signals

00:01:07.310 --> 00:01:09.789
even if I'm not really doing anything at all.

00:01:09.789 --> 00:01:12.139
That's why it's usable for people who are
actually paralysed

00:01:12.139 --> 00:01:15.389
or suffer from other types of illnesses.

00:01:15.389 --> 00:01:18.280
We're not actually reading someone's mind
but we're measuring their brain signals.

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:20.770
We have a cap, which has 64 electrodes

00:01:20.770 --> 00:01:23.709
to actually measure those brain signals
through the skull,

00:01:23.709 --> 00:01:26.060
and through a special gel that we put in.

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:28.409
The cap is actually quite comfortable
once it's on.

00:01:28.409 --> 00:01:31.109
You don't actually feel it anymore so it's
just measuring there.

00:01:31.109 --> 00:01:34.620
It's not going to bother them.
At least we hope it's not!

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:37.400
In theory, getting a negative result is
a good thing.

00:01:37.409 --> 00:01:40.779
And for the BrainFly team, that would be
brain activity in microgravity

00:01:40.779 --> 00:01:42.929
being too different for the system to detect.

00:01:42.929 --> 00:01:47.090
Now, as long as the equipment worked,
that isn't a failure.

00:01:47.090 --> 00:01:49.270
That just means your idea
has been proven wrong.

00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:50.780
That's still a step forward.

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:54.459
But, in practice, humans don't work that way.

00:01:54.459 --> 00:01:56.439
It's called publication bias.

00:01:56.439 --> 00:01:59.179
Negative results are less likely
to be written up by scientists,

00:01:59.179 --> 00:02:01.049
less likely to be published by journals

00:02:01.049 --> 00:02:04.369
and less likely to make it out
to the rest of the world.

00:02:04.369 --> 00:02:07.709
The media doesn't care if nothing's changed.

00:02:07.709 --> 00:02:09.799
Scientific institutions have limited money

00:02:09.799 --> 00:02:13.579
and getting flashy, amazing results that generate
lots of press coverage...

00:02:13.579 --> 00:02:15.390
oh, that's good for your career.

00:02:15.390 --> 00:02:17.620
So if you're putting all that
time and money in,

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:23.000
there is still a stigma that, if you get a
negative result, you have somehow failed.

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:24.159
We do hope to publish.

00:02:24.159 --> 00:02:28.050
We hope to get, maybe at least,
four papers actually,

00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:30.170
because we all have
different thesis subjects on it.

00:02:30.170 --> 00:02:32.420
In the best case scenario,
everything works perfectly.

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:38.940
The BCI's still controllable, the data's not noisy,
all nine participants give perfect data.

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:41.020
That's the dream result.

00:02:41.030 --> 00:02:47.750
My ideal result is actually this little tiny
brain signal called a P300,

00:02:47.750 --> 00:02:51.400
which is a positive peak
in your electrical signal.

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:54.349
So what I think and what I hope is that microgravity,

00:02:54.349 --> 00:03:00.410
the moment when you're weightless, may result
in a slight cognitive enhancement.

00:03:00.410 --> 00:03:03.480
So up there, in those 20 second bursts of
microgravity,

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:06.319
those researchers tried to disprove their
null hypothesis.

00:03:06.319 --> 00:03:10.959
For BrainFly, that is: is our brain activity
comparable in zero G?

00:03:10.959 --> 00:03:13.650
Can you still use a brain-computer interface
if you're in orbit?

00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:19.829
In a few decades, could astronauts doing space
walks use their minds to control equipment?

00:03:19.829 --> 00:03:21.230
I'm not going to tell you.

00:03:21.230 --> 00:03:22.780
You can look it up when
they all publish their papers

00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:26.299
because the achievement is the same,
regardless of the results.

00:03:26.299 --> 00:03:28.460
Science has taken one small step forward,

00:03:28.460 --> 00:03:31.520
no matter whether they disprove
their null hypothesis or not.

00:03:33.500 --> 00:03:36.520
Nah, I'm just kidding, they got the results
they wanted.

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:43.700
If you're a masters or PhD student from an
ESA member state,

00:03:43.700 --> 00:03:45.569
and you want to be
one of the people behind me,

00:03:45.569 --> 00:03:47.730
then have a look at the Fly Your Thesis program.

00:03:47.730 --> 00:03:49.840
The link is in the description!

